ny  communities they have worked closely  with the local businesses to try and  figure out what are the skill sets that  are most valued by those businesses  they're more responsive to the  marketplace and so a lot of times  community colleges can be a wonderful  place to to improve improve your skills  and by the way Brian that is one of the  I talked about improving your skills one  of the key points this is in chapter 8  of this book is that you really have to  change the way you think about your  career that in the new economy you  constantly have to be working on  upgrading your skills upgrading your  attractiveness upgrading your value in  in the marketplace buying stocks what  would you do buying stocks first of all  it depends on how much time and energy  you want to invest in it if you don't  want to invest a lot of time and energy  stock index funds are a good way to go  more often than not you'll do better  than the money managers by doing that  what about savings you have some figures  in here about our little American say  it's their astonishing figures and I  also provide a link to smart money calm  which has a little savings account a  little savings calculator that can help  you figure out how much you need to save  to achieve your goals in retirement when  you wrote this there were 250  billionaire's I've seen a list somewhere  there's over 300 billionaire's the  United States and four million  millionaires in 1998 what impact does  that have on everything a an amazing  creation of wealth in a very in a very  short time and one of the disturbing  aspects of the new economy I believe  strongly on balance that the new economy  is a good thing for the vast majority of  people but one of the disturbing aspects  as there has been a growing gap between  the rich and the poor both in this  country and worldwide rich nations and  poor nations  her farther apart than they were 20 or  30 years ago I don't think that has to  be so I think there's some signs that  it's starting to turn around in this  country but I think it is one of the  very disturbing side effects of what's  been going on for the last couple did  you change your mind about anything  during this writing of this book about  how you live your own life oh yeah oh  yeah I changed a lot of things as I told  you earlier one of the things that I  wasn't prepared for was the degree to  which all prices have gone flexible in  this economy that you never pay the  sticker price I didn't know that I  wasn't aware that and it has changed  that has certainly changed the way I  operate I'm also increasingly convinced  that the value of spending just a little  bit of time using the Internet to do  research before making any kind of a  major purchase or an investment decision  just an awful lot of information out out  there that can be incredibly useful  early in your career you took two trips  at least two he took three basically  went 1-2 Russia 12 pole and one to Japan  what did what were those about well the  Russian trip I went right after the  collapse of the Communist Party and you  know I grew up at a time when we were  still doing civil defense drills under  our desks in elementary school because  of the fear of the Russians and it was  an amazing thing to see what a basket  case the centrally planned Russian  economy was to realize what a total  failure this system had ultimately  provided there was a joke in Moscow at  the time I was there about the guy whose  car broke down and he needed a new car  and he so he called up and they said yes  sir we can bring you a car but it'll be  on May the fifth in the year two  thousand and four and the guy says well  could you make it in the afternoon i  have a plumber coming that morning it  was sort of a sign of just the total  collapse of that economy the japanese  experience was very different again  remember 10 years ago there was a sense  that maybe the Japanese had found the  third  way that maybe the Japanese had 